# Hibernian lore, myths and exploration talk (classic + Shrouded Isles). New writing (see docs/bot-voices).
# Facts from the zone and patch lore are said plainly; player rumours, superstitions and invented myths (the door
# under the hill, the sleeping Fianna, drop myths) are said as belief, memory or hearsay. No bot here claims a kill
# of a level 70+ boss. Flame and the old Muire lord are not named (not in this world / by request).

@kind ambient
@realm hib
@topic lore
@about place

# ---------------- Sheeroe, the Glimmer King and the Azure ----------------
@who *
@levels *
a pass came out of the bog of cullen's mist one day and nobody had ever seen it before. that is how sheeroe began
the rangers who found the sheeroe pass swore it had not been there the week before
sheeroe is an otherworld kingdom and cuuldurach is its king. we are the guests up there, not the other way round
the glimmers glow because the otherworld leaks through them, or so the old sages tell it
the azure are firbolg kin who pray to the sky, and they want every glimmer in sheeroe burned out
[funny how|odd that] the azure hate the glimmers more than they hate [us|the albs]
never count the glimmers out loud in sheeroe. my [old guild|first guild] swore it [draws the prince|brings maldahar] down on you
[i like to sit|sometimes i sit] on the sheeroe ridge and watch the glimmers drift. you can almost see the otherworld behind them
the azure preach at the glimmers all day and the glimmers just keep glowing. patience of a saint

@levels 35-50
cuuldurach the glimmer king, [eighty levels of|a whole hillside of] light and teeth. [seen him once from the ridge|seen him from far off] and that was plenty
they say cuuldurach is the only male dragon of the three. the norse argue about theirs
[heard|word is] the glimmer king [takes heat badly now|cannot stand fire anymore]. [our eldritch are smug about it|the casters are thrilled]
the glimmer king drops a pile of respec stones. most raids go up for the stones, not the armour
people say the dragon drops nothing but coin. those people have never been on a raid
old raid advice i heard: fight the glimmer king up close. stand off at range and his breath finds the whole ridge
do not nuke a dragon early. let the tanks hold him first or he turns on the casters, every raid leader says so

@who Explorer
@levels *
there is a fold in the bog of cullen where the mist never lifts. i keep thinking another pass will open there one day
the mist in the bog of cullen moves against the wind. [i have watched it|watch it some time]. it is looking for something

# ---------------- Breifine, Evern and the tunnels ----------------
@who *
@levels 35-50
[they say|my guild leader always said] the fairies that land on evern's stones heal him. kill the fairies first
evern is seventy something and every fairy around him counts as a healer. no thanks
i think evern is older than the frontier keeps. the stones were there first and the keeps went up around them

@levels *
there are tunnels under breifine full of undead. somebody carved them, and nobody is saying who
heard the marfach caverns serve some power that wants all three realms on its knees. cheerful neighbours
leave a bit of bread on the stones in breifine before a raid. my guild never skips it

@who Daredevil
dare: walk into evern's stone ring, touch one stone and run. nobody has taken me up yet

@who Explorer
counted the stones around evern once. got twenty three, a friend got twenty two. the fairies moved one

# ---------------- The Cursed Forest ----------------
@who *
@levels *
the faerie court is said to walk the cursed forest. i have seen lights there that were not wisps
if something in the cursed forest calls you by name, do not answer. [old hib rule|my first guild's only rule]
everyone knows the cursed forest for the werewolf camps, but the deep woods hold older things
skeaghshee means fairy thorn, i think. leave the thorn trees alone, is all i am saying
never cut a thorn tree in hibernia. the folk under the hills remember

@levels 40-50
luriquay [walks|haunts] the cursed forest and he [is not as easy as he looks|has ended more groups than the werewolves]
people swear the con colours lie about juggernaut. i am not testing it
i bet even the faerie court steps around juggernaut when he comes through

@who Daredevil
dare for the brave: stand still in the cursed forest when something calls your name. i did it once

@who Crafter
old crafter belief: anything you loot in the cursed forest carries a curse until it has been sold twice

# ---------------- Muire Tomb ----------------
@who *
@levels *
the old lord of the muire house went mad and killed his whole household. it has not been quiet since
every hib has done muire tomb at some point. every hib has also heard something behind them in there
[don't|never] whistle in muire tomb. [my guild swears|an old druid told me] it wakes [the family|the muires] up
muire tomb feels sad more than scary. a whole family laid down there because of what their lord did
the muire hero still guards his kin down there. loyal even now, i suppose

@who Leveler
@levels 10-30
muire tomb is decent xp that nobody wants, too gloomy for most. suits me fine

# ---------------- Firbolg, Tuatha, siabra and the old names ----------------
@who *
@levels *
the firbolg ruled this land first, before the tuatha came. every firbolg i know will tell you so twice
they say tir na nog was the tuatha's refuge when the world turned on them. now it is full of merchants
the siabra are elf kin gone wrong, and they hold more of hibernia than people like to admit
the old songs say we warred with the folk under the hills before we made peace against the norse and the britons
three of our towns carry otherworld names. mag mell, tir na mbeo, tir na nog. [i do not think that is chance|somebody knew something]
the tuatha came in on a cloud of mist, the old songs say. maybe that is the bog of cullen's mist
the firbolg say they were here when the hills were young. the tuatha just smile at that
the siabra wayguards watch the roads like they still own the land. maybe they do
the overseer of the silver hand in coruscating mine. silver hand, like nuada. that is no accident
queen cliodna sits in the bog of cullen. in the old tales her birds sing people to sleep. i keep my distance
the amadan dubh in connacht, the dark fool. one touch and you are never right again, [or so the old folk say|my gran said]
the grey man walks the cliffs of moher in the fog. sailors say he is the fog
badb nests in the bog of cullen, a crow of war. the old folk say she smells battles coming
the song of fionnuala and her brothers turned to swans still gets me. every bard in hibernia knows it
the dullahan rides when someone is about to die. [hear hooves and see no horse, run|hooves with no horse means go home]
heard a banshee wail right before a pull once. [we wiped|someone died], of course. [i do not pull after a wail now|lesson learned]
earthquakes caved in part of the spraggon den and the spraggons picked a new leader. even spraggons have politics
coruscating mine was not open when hibernia was young, the veterans say. makes you wonder what else is shut

# ---------------- The hidden door and the sleeping Fianna ----------------
there is a door under a hill somewhere in hibernia that opens into the otherworld. sheeroe proves the doors are real
[my grandmother|an old bard in ardee] said the door to the otherworld opens only when the mist comes up from the bog
the fianna never died. they sleep under a hill in connacht, waiting for someone to wake them
[the bards say|an old bard told me] a horn hangs inside the fianna's hill. blow it three times and they wake
if the fianna wake i want to be there. [imagine fionn's men|imagine them] at emain macha against the norse
samhain is when the doors open, the old folk say. [i plan to be on a hill in connacht that night|i will be in bri leith with a lantern]
if hy brasil was hidden all those years, the otherworld door can be hidden too
hy brasil was a children's story until the sylvan came asking for help. makes you wonder what else is real
the melancholic fairy queen sits by lough gur. [i think she is locked out of her own door|maybe she lost the way home]
there is a story that a rider sleeps under lough gur and comes out once every seven years
my guild's old saying: the otherworld is never far, it is just one step sideways

@who Explorer
bri leith was a fairy hill before it was a valley. i think the door is there
i have walked every hill in bri leith knocking on stones. still nothing. i will keep knocking
i asked every bard in tir na nog about the sleeping fianna. each one put the hill somewhere else in connacht
my old map shows a hill in connacht with no name on it. the new maps leave it off entirely
there is a hill in connacht where the wind goes quiet. stood on it once and [felt someone waiting below|my horse would not stay]
rode the whole connacht coast with a lantern looking for a door in the hills. found a lot of spiders instead
stood in a fairy ring in lough derg for a full minute. nothing happened. maybe it was the wrong night
i mark every cave i find on my map in ink. connacht has more holes in it than the map admits
[my granda|an old fisherman in connla] said you could see hy brasil from the cliffs of moher once every seven years. nobody laughs now

@who Socialite
heard a guild found an old door in a connacht hillside. nobody will say where. probably nothing
gossip says someone blew a horn in connacht and the hill [answered|rumbled]. [i do not believe a word|sounds like ale talking]
a bard in tir na nog swears she knows where the fianna sleep. [she wants gold for it|she charges a round of drinks per hint]

# ---------------- Edges of the map ----------------
@who Explorer
swam west off the cliffs of moher until [the shore was gone|i lost sight of land]. the sea just keeps going, and so does the fog
world's end is a real zone. i went to see if the world [actually ends|really stops] there. [it nearly does|close enough]
the shannon estuary goes further out than the map shows. swam it once and the water just turned grey
the old maps leave the sea past the cliffs blank. i think the map maker just gave up
climbed the highest rock on vigilant rock just to see the sea. [fog and more fog|nothing out there], and something big moving under it
every new island they find is one we called a myth. [hy brasil, allta fearann|first hy brasil, now allta fearann]. [i read old maps differently now|i stopped laughing at old maps]
allta fearann turned up out of nowhere. one more place nobody knew about. the map keeps growing
[i swear|i am sure] there is a cave mouth on the shannon side nobody talks about. went twenty steps in and turned back
before hy brasil, the edge of the map was the edge of the world. now i keep looking past it

@who Daredevil
someone should swim out past world's end and see what is there. [i will if two more come|i need a lot of company for that]
dare: run straight through the glimmer camps in sheeroe without stopping. [first one to the far ridge wins|loser buys the next drink]

# ---------------- Hy Brasil: Olcasgean, Galladoria, Fomor, Balor ----------------
@who *
@levels 30-50
olcasgean was banished to world's end, and now his roots poison hy brasil. [the trees out there look sick|you can see the rot]
olcasgean is a child of gaea, [older than the tuatha|older than any of us], and he has been sulking at the edge of the world ever since
galladoria is where the roots lead. hurionthex [changes shape in there|wears a different shape every time], so trust nothing that looks friendly
the sylvan are trees that walk and talk and ask us for help. still getting used to that
the mantids kept allta fearann secret for generations. their untamed land, they call it
heard curengkur stalks allta fearann and the mantids only say the name in a whisper
balor of the evil eye holds tur suil. [never meet his eye|do not look him in the eye], [if half the stories are true|the stories say]
i think the fomorians were here before anyone. balor's eye has seen every age of hibernia
myrddraxis is a hydra at the bottom of fomor, and they say the heads heal each other
heard of a raid of fifty that fought myrddraxis for an hour and came out with nothing but [repair bills|bruises]
balor's eye opened once and a whole valley burned, the sylvan say. that is why the vale of balor looks like that
norsob the annihilator lives in tur suil. with a name like that, i am not asking questions
elro the ancient waits out at world's end. i believe the name

@who Crafter
myrddraxis is supposed to drop a scale that never breaks. never seen one myself
glimmer dust from sheeroe is supposed to [make a dye that glows|shine armour like new]. my vendor did not agree
people say the respec stones from the glimmer king are worth more than all the armour he drops

# ---------------- Relics ----------------
@who *
@levels *
lug's spear rests in cruachan and the dagda's cauldron in mount collory. [the albs only borrow them|the norse only ever borrow them]
the dagda's cauldron never runs empty in the old tales. that explains our soldiers
lug's spear [never misses|throws itself] in the stories. [good thing it is on our side|i hope it remembers that in a siege]
they say there were meant to be more than six relics. [i think one is hidden in hibernia|my guess is one is under a hill here]
my old guild swore nuada's sword was meant to be our third relic and just never turned up
the stone of fal is out there somewhere, the bards say. it would cry out under a true king, if anyone ever found it

# ---------------- Bards ----------------
@class Bard
people say bards are overpowered. we [mez|mez, heal] and run fast. that is called [being useful|talent]
@class *
every realm thinks our bards are overpowered. they are not wrong, and we are not giving them up
a bard with speed, mez and a heal is three classes in one kilt. [overpowered|unfair], and i love it

# ---------------- Veterans remember ----------------
@who NoLifer
@levels 40-50
i remember when sheeroe first opened. half the realm ran up the pass just to look at the glimmers
my first night in tir na nog i got lost for an hour. the city felt like it went on forever
i still remember the first time the cursed forest werewolves trained my group. never went back without a bard
veterans told us the realm was bigger than the map. turned out they were right
remember when hy brasil was just a word in a song? [now i have a bind point there|now people level there]
the first time i saw a sylvan move i swung at it. it was very polite about it

# ---------------- Gossip and dares ----------------
@who Socialite
@levels *
rumour in tir na nog is the azure are recruiting. bring snacks
someone in ardee swears the melancholic fairy queen [smiled at her|waved at her]. [i do not buy it|sure she did]

# ---------------- Is it worth it ----------------
@who Leveler
@levels 30-50
sheeroe is good xp if you can stand the glow. the azure camps never run dry
hy brasil is lovely, but i still level faster in the old zones. pretty does not pay
lore is nice and all but i go to sheeroe for xp, not for the otherworld
@about

# =========================================================================================
# Debates (second pass: long scenes, the old Irish tales set against the game's own lore)
# Documented zone and patch lore is said plainly; the old tales are cited as "the old tale"; inventions are belief.
# =========================================================================================
@kind debate
@realm hib

# ---- Flagship: the door to the Otherworld under a hill --------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_door
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_hidden_door
@role B type=Explorer
@role C type=NoLifer,Leveler levels=35-50
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Socialite,Daredevil
A: {q} does anyone else think there is a real door to the otherworld under one of our hills? | {q} i keep coming back to it: somewhere under a hill in hibernia there is a door to the otherworld
?D: wait, the otherworld? like tir na nog? i bind there, it is just a city | otherworld as in tir na nog the capital? i thought that was just where the trainers are
B: the old tale says when the milesians came, the tuatha went under the hills into the sidhe mounds. the land of youth was down there | in the old story the tuatha lost the land to the milesians and went down under the hills. tir na nog, the land of youth, was below
C: and the realm named its capital after it, and two towns, mag mell and tir na mbeo. names on a map are not doors | we named a city tir na nog and a town mag mell. that is a realm that likes old songs, not proof of a hole in a hill
B: read the realm history again. it says the celts warred with the folk under the hills before the alliance. not a figure of speech | the realm's own story: the celts fought the folk under the hills, then made peace with them against the norse and the britons
C: and those folk came out and they are the elves at the next table. whatever door they used, they shut it behind them | right, and they walked out and joined us. elves buy bread in ardee now. whatever door they came through, it was theirs and it is shut
B: then explain sheeroe. rangers came out of the cullen mist onto a pass that had not been there, and the sages called it an otherworld kingdom | sheeroe. a pass came out of the bog mist that the rangers swore was never there, and the sages said it led into an otherworld kingdom
C: i rode up that pass the first week. it is a road with grass on it. a door you can ride a horse through is called a valley | i was up there the week it opened. stones, grass, glimmers. if that is a door then every gap in silvermine is one too
B: a valley no ranger had mapped the week before, with a king of light at the top no bard had sung of. somebody left a door open | a valley that was not on any ranger's map, ruled by a glimmer king nobody had heard of. a door was left open and we walked in
B: think how the tuatha came. the old tale says they came in dark clouds, landed on a mountain in connacht, and mist hid the sun three days | the tuatha arrived in a mist so thick the sun was gone for three days, and came down on a mountain in connacht. mist, every time
C: every bog in this realm has mist. by that rule there is a door in every puddle between ardee and the shannon | cullen has mist every morning. lough derg has mist. my bath has mist. you would have a door in every puddle
B: the mist is the point. the sea god gave the tuatha the feth fiada, a veil that hid them from mortal eyes. that is not weather, it is a lock | the old tales say the tuatha were given the feth fiada, a magic mist that hid them from mortal men. hiding in mist is the whole trick
D: ok, so if there is a door, where would it even be? | so where would you even look? hibernia is big
B: bri leith. in the old tale bri leith is midir's own mound, the hill he flew out of with etain as two swans. the valley kept his hill's name | bri leith first. in the old stories it was midir's mound, where he carried etain off as a swan. a fairy hill's name on a whole valley
B: and look who lives in it: guardian hru, keeper rasa, watcher rylie. guardian of what? keeper of what? watching for whom? | and the named in that valley: guardian hru, keeper rasa, watcher rylie. three of them, all set to guard or watch something
C: niy the overseer lives there too. if every overseer guarded a door, the coruscating mine would be the gate of the gods | half the named in this realm are watchers and overseers. titles are cheap, the coruscating mine has three of them
?E: there is also something called lusmorebane in bri leith. sounds like a herb with a grudge | lusmorebane. i always figured that was a gardener who lost a fight
B: lusmore is foxglove, the fairy plant. the old folk said it belonged to the sidhe. something in that valley is named for killing it | lusmore is the old name for foxglove, the fairy's own flower. a thing called the bane of the fairy plant, in a fairy valley. odd, no?
~ 15
A: my old guild's bard said the real gate is not in bri leith but at cruachan. the old tales put a cave of cats there | i heard a different one. the old tales put the gate of the otherworld at cruachan, in the cave of cats
A: in the tale things came out of that cave every samhain. and where do we keep lug's spear? cruachan. think about it | the cave of cats opened every samhain and the otherworld spilled out. and that is where our spear rests, of all places
C: cruachan is a gorge full of albs every relic night. if there were a door to the land of youth in it, an alb would have fallen in by now | the albs have stomped through cruachan more times than i can count. none of them ever came back young and confused
B: samhain is when the mounds open, all the tales agree. nera went into the cruachan cave on samhain and lived a year in the sidhe | in the tale nera walked into that cave on samhain, married in the sidhe and came back out with summer fruit in the middle of winter
C: and nera came back seeing cruachan in flames, and the queen raided the mound before it could happen. a war story, not a travel guide | read the end of nera's tale. it is a war between cruachan and the mound. nobody tells it to children for a reason
B: i have knocked on every hill in bri leith and sat by lough gur till dark. the melancholic fairy queen there looks like someone locked out | every hill in bri leith, by hand. and i sat near the melancholic fairy queen at lough gur a long while. she looks like she lost her key
C: she is level sixty seven and wipes groups that sit too close. mournful and locked out are not the same thing | she cons purple to me and she has killed more explorers than any door ever has
E: dare: first one through the door has to explain to the guards in tir na nog why they are three hundred years late | whoever finds it, try not to come home three hundred years late. the stablemaster will want back pay
C: that is oisin's tale, and the best reason not to go. three years in the land of youth, three hundred gone, then he touched the ground | think about oisin. three years with niamh, home to find the fianna dust, and the moment he touched the ground he was an old man
B: so i stay in the saddle. oisin aged because his girth broke and he fell. the door was never his problem | then i do not get off the horse. oisin's mistake was the saddle girth, not the door
A: samhain, bri leith, a lantern each, and nobody gets off their horse. i am in | ok. samhain, bri leith, lanterns, and we all stay mounted. sign me up
C: send me a tell from inside. if it arrives three hundred years late i will know you were right | if a hill opens, send a tell. i will be the old man at the bind stone waiting for it

# ---- The Fianna asleep under a hill ------------------------------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_fianna
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_fianna
@role B class=Bard
@role C type=NoLifer,Leveler levels=30-50
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Socialite,Daredevil
A: {q} the bards keep singing that the fianna never died, they only sleep under a hill in connacht | {q} heard another bard tonight swear the fianna are asleep under some hill in connacht
?D: who are the fianna? is that a guild? | sorry, the fianna? are they a guild or a camp?
B: fionn mac cumhaill's war band, the best fighters this land ever had. they kept the coasts for the high king and hunted the whole land | fionn's men. a war band that guarded the high king, hunted with the great hounds bran and sceolang, and kept the coasts
B: you could not even join unless you ran the forest with men chasing you and broke no dry twig and loosened no braid | to join you leapt a branch as high as your brow, ducked one as low as your knee, and pulled a thorn from your foot at full run
E: so roughly the same as getting into my guild, minus the twig | my guild only asks that you show up to the keep raid sober. lower bar
C: lovely songs. but there is not one fianna anything in this realm. no camp, no quest, no stone with fionn's name on it | i have played this realm since the first week and never seen a sign of them. not a quest, not a statue, not a named
B: a sleeping army does not hire a merchant. the tale says they lie in a cave in rows, with a horn hung by the entrance | of course not. a sleeping army does not put up a sign. the old tale is clear: a cave, the warriors in rows, a horn by the door
B: a herdsman found it once and blew the horn twice. they rose up on their elbows, he ran, and nobody ever blew the third blast | the story goes a man found them, blew twice, and they lifted up on their elbows staring at him. he fled before the third note
C: every bard puts that cave somewhere else. i have heard bri leith, the connacht hills, under lough gur, and one fellow said howth | which hill, though? i was told connacht, bri leith and lough gur by three bards in the same evening in tir na nog
B: a hill that wants to stay hidden does not keep one address. that is how hiding works | if the hill held still for every bard it would not be much of a secret
D: say someone did wake them. would they even fight for us? | if they woke up, whose side would they take?
B: half their songs are about throwing the men of lochlann back into the sea, and lochlann is the norse. they know whose side they are on | ask the songs. the fianna spent half their days fighting the men of lochlann. lochlann is the norse
C: they also turned on the high king in the end. the battle of gabhra, where oscar fell and the fianna were broken. they fight for themselves | and the end of the songs is gabhra, fianna against the high king's men, oscar dead on the field. loyal to their own band, nobody else
E: speaking of oscar, there is a named oscar in the vale of balor. maybe the fianna are awake already and camping hy brasil | funny thing, there is a named called oscar out in the vale of balor. maybe he woke early and wandered off
C: that oscar is a fifty five named next to one called milo. i do not think fionn's grandson keeps that company | the vale of balor oscar camps beside something called milo. fionn's grandson would keep better company than that
~ 15
A: what i want to know is why it is always connacht | why does it always come back to connacht?
B: the tuatha came down on a mountain in connacht, and after the first mag tuired the firbolg were given connacht to keep. old dead, old ground | connacht is where the old peoples kept their last ground. the firbolg got connacht after the first mag tuired. old dead sleep in old ground
C: connacht also has the amadan dubh walking about. one touch of the dark fool and you are never right again. not a hill for horn blowing | if you blow a horn in connacht what answers is the amadan dubh, and one touch of the dark fool takes your wits for good
E: dare stands: whoever finds the hill blows three times, and the rest of us stand behind with the rez | i will hold the horn if someone else holds the healer
B: an old bard in ardee taught me the notes of the dord fiann, fionn's own hunting call. one day i play it on the right hill | i learned the dord fiann call from an old bard in ardee. i will play it on every hill in connacht until one answers
?D: can i come? i want to see a hill wake up | if it works, can i watch?
C: if a hill sits up at your music i will buy every bard in tir na nog a drink | if a hill answers your song, i will carry your harp for a month

# ---- The Glimmer King, the only male dragon? ---------------------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_dragon
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_male_dragon
@role B type=NoLifer levels=40-50
@role C type=Explorer
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Socialite,Daredevil
A: {q} is the glimmer king really the only male dragon of the three? | {q} people say cuuldurach is the only he among the realm dragons. anyone believe that?
?D: the glimmer king? i have only seen glimmers, the little glowing things in sheeroe | who is the glimmer king? i know glimmers but not a king
B: cuuldurach. eighty levels of light and teeth at the far end of the sheeroe hills. the glimmers you kill on the slopes are his court | cuuldurach the glimmer king. the glimmers are his subjects, he sits at the far end of sheeroe, and it takes a raid of fifty to look at him
C: and he is not even from here. the sages said sheeroe is an otherworld kingdom. he is a king of the folk under the hills who is also a dragon | sheeroe is a corner of the otherworld that came out of the mist. he is a fairy king first and a dragon second, if you ask me
B: every raid leader i have heard on the ridge calls him he. the albs call their stone dragon she, and the norse cannot agree on their wolf | the raid talk always says he. golestandt is a she to every alb, and midgard argues about gjalpinulva like it is a hobby
D: why would anyone care whether a dragon is a he or a she? | does it matter at all?
E: it matters if you plan to ask one to dance | only if you plan to marry it, and i have seen what you call a date
C: it matters for the title. he is king, not queen, and maldahar the glimmer prince sits below him. a king and a prince make a family | a glimmer king, and a glimmer prince called maldahar in the same hills. that is a father and a son, a plain line of succession
B: a prince only proves a royal house. queens have princes too. maldahar could be anybody's boy | princes come from queens as often as kings. that proves a family, not a father
C: when the dragons came in they were named by kind: wolf-headed, stone and glimmer. glimmer is a court, not a beast. kings rule courts | the dragons came named by kind, wolf-headed, stone and glimmer. only ours came with a crown on
B: the old tales have few dragons here anyway. the great serpent of lough derg was a she, the mother of demons, and fionn killed her | the one great serpent i know from the old tales is the caoranach in lough derg, the mother of demons. a she, and fionn slew her
D: wait, there is a dragon in lough derg too? i level there | in lough derg? i level there, i have only seen the small stuff
C: only in the old story. the lough turned red with her blood, they say, and derg means red. you will meet bandits there, nothing with wings | not one you will meet. it is the tale of how the lough got its name. derg means red, for her blood
B: point is, he is the odd one in every way. he takes heat worse since that change, and the raids go up for his respec stones, not his armour | odd in every way. he took more heat after the change, and like the other two he drops a pile of respec stones the raids really come for
E: so he is male, he hates fire and he gives away stones. sounds like my uncle | a moody old man who hates heat and hands out stones. i have met him at weddings
~ 15
C: the sheeroe lore itself calls the kingdom his and calls him king. the sages were not guessing | i will trust the sages. they named the otherworld kingdom and its ruler, and the ruler is a king
B: the same sages never mentioned the breath. i trust them on names and nothing else | sages say plenty. none of them ever stood on the ridge when he breathed
A: so the answer is that nobody has checked | so nobody really knows
B: nobody has checked and nobody is going to. you do not walk up to eighty levels of breath and ask | no raid leader has ever asked him, and i am not volunteering
?E: dare: next raid somebody opens with excuse me, sir or madam, and we see what he breathes on first | i dare the next raid leader to call him your majesty and see if he blushes
C: i will watch from the ridge and call him he until someone proves otherwise | he it is, then. and i keep my distance from his breath either way

# ---- Evern and the stones of Breifine ----------------------------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_evern
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_evern_stones
@role B type=Daredevil levels=35-50
@role C type=NoLifer levels=40-50
@role D class=Druid
@role E type=* levels=5-25
A: {q} what is the real trick to evern? every story i hear is different | {q} three people have told me how to fight evern and none of them agree
?E: who is evern? | evern? is that a person or a place?
C: a big named in breifine, our own frontier. seventy something, standing stones all round him, and fairies flying in to land on them | a named of seventy plus in breifine, among a field of standing stones, and fairies that keep coming to sit on the stones
B: and every fairy that lands on a stone heals him. so you kill the fairies first, every one, before they touch down | the trick is fairies first. each one that sits on a stone heals him, so the whole raid shoots fairies before anyone hits evern
C: that is the story everyone repeats. an old raid leader told me the stones do the healing and the fairies only wake them | i heard it the other way round: the stones heal him, the fairies just switch them on
B: then why do the fairies go straight for the stones and nowhere else? | the stones sit there doing nothing until a fairy lands. that is your answer
D: druid view: no healer heals forever alone. something feeds the healer. here the stones are the well and the fairies are the hands | speaking as a healer, power has to come from somewhere. the fairies are the casters, the stones are what they draw on
E: why would a stone heal anyone though? | how can a rock heal something?
D: in the old tales stones remember. the stone of fal at tara cried out under a true king. stones in this land were never only stones | the old stories are full of stones that know things. the stone of fal shouted when the rightful king stood on it
C: my count from the hill was twenty three stones. a druid i trust got twenty one. either someone cannot count or the field shifts | i counted twenty three once, and the next week a friend got twenty one and swore she counted twice
B: or she counted a dead ranger as a stone. happens out there | half the stones in breifine are albs lying very still
~ 15
A: how old are those stones anyway? were they there before the keeps? | were the stones in breifine before the frontier keeps went up?
C: older, i think. the keeps went up around them. and under breifine run the marfach caverns, undead tunnels nobody will admit to carving | older for sure. and below breifine are the marfach caverns, tunnels full of undead that were found after the frontiers were built
D: those tunnels serve some power that wants all three realms on their knees, the story goes. maybe evern sits on the lid | the caverns serve a third power that wants every realm down, they say. maybe evern and his stones are a seal on top of it
B: so killing evern lets the third power out? great, more things to kill | so if we drop evern, something worse climbs up? sounds like xp to me
C: or evern is a big named with good healers and you two are writing songs | or he is a big named with fairy healers, and nobody needs a ballad about it
D: in the old tales farmers ploughed around fairy forts and never cut a lone thorn. i would leave those stones be even if i could break them | the old folk never broke a fairy ring or cut the lone thorn. i would not smash those stones even if we could
B: next raid i stand on a stone myself. no fairy lands on a stone with me on it | then i stand on a stone during the fight. let a fairy try to land on my head
C: tell me how it goes from the bind point | i will watch from the hill and count you as stone twenty four

# ---- Who was in Hibernia first -----------------------------------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_oldest
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_who_was_first
@role B type=Explorer
@role C type=Socialite
@role D type=NoLifer levels=35-50
@role E type=* levels=5-25
A: {q} which came first in hibernia, the firbolg or the tuatha? my firbolg friend will not drop it | {q} settle a guild argument: who was in hibernia first, firbolg or tuatha?
B: the firbolg. the realm history says it plainly, the firbolg ruled this land before the tuatha came | the firbolg, and not just by their own say. the realm story itself says they ruled before the tuatha arrived
?E: who are the tuatha? i know firbolg, i play one | tuatha? i rolled a firbolg and nobody told me about them
B: the tuatha de danann, the people of danu. the old tale says they came in dark clouds with four treasures and their magic, and took the land | the old tale's gods of this land: the people of danu. they came in mist and cloud carrying four treasures and took the land off the firbolg
C: took it how, politely? | i bet the firbolg loved that
B: at the first battle of mag tuired. nuada their king lost his arm there and wore a silver one after. the firbolg lost and kept connacht | the first battle of mag tuired. the firbolg lost, nuada lost his arm and got a silver one, and the firbolg were left connacht
D: and before both of them were the fomorians, raiders from the sea. the second mag tuired was tuatha against fomor, with balor at their head | both late. the fomorians were here before anyone, sea raiders led by balor. the second mag tuired was fought against them
E: balor like the vale of balor in hy brasil? | wait, balor as in tur suil balor?
D: the same name. the old tale says lugh killed him, but nobody told the one sitting in tur suil | the same, and alive by the look of tur suil. the sylvan say his eye burned a whole valley once
B: the fomorians lost in the tale and went off to islands nobody could find. and where do we meet them? on hy brasil, the isle called a myth | the fomor lost and went to their islands. now we fight them on an isle that was only a story. that fits the tale rather well
C: so the firbolg were first, except for the fomorians, who were first except for whoever came before them. my head hurts | firbolg first, unless fomor, unless someone else. this is worse than a keep timer argument
~ 15
D: if you want the real oldest, it is olcasgean out at world's end. a titan, a child of gaea, old before any of these had names | the oldest thing we know of is olcasgean, the titan banished to world's end. a child of gaea, older than the tuatha and the fomor both
B: gaea is not even a name from our tales. if he is a child of gaea he came from somewhere far off. a titan in exile is a visitor | gaea is a name from the far southern stories, not ours. an exiled titan is a guest, a very old and rude guest
D: banished to the edge of the world is still here. his roots poison the whole of hy brasil, so he is here enough | banished or not, his roots run under the whole isle. something that old does not need anyone's permission
?E: what about the siabra? they act like they own the roads | the siabra wayguards act like the land is theirs. are they older?
C: ask a siabra and they were always here. ask a lurikeen and he was here first, only too small for anyone to notice | every siabra claims all of it. every lurikeen claims he was here first but nobody looked down
B: the land was here first. even the firbolg came carrying earth in bags, the old tale says, so they knew good ground when they saw it | the hills were older than all of them. the firbolg were the men of the bags, carrying earth with them. they respected land
A: i will tell my firbolg friend he won, with a footnote the size of balor | so firbolg first, with a footnote about fomor, titans and the rest. he will take that
D: tell him the fomorians want their land back. that should keep him quiet for a week | tell him from me that balor sends his regards

# ---- The Azure crusade --------------------------------------------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_azure
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_azure_crusade
@role B type=Daredevil,Socialite
@role C type=Explorer
@role D type=Leveler levels=25-50
@role E type=* levels=5-25
A: {q} are the azure right to want every glimmer burned out of sheeroe? | {q} serious question, should hibernia back the azure crusade or leave sheeroe be?
?E: the azure, the blue firbolg up in sheeroe? i thought they were just mobs | who are the azure? the ones preaching at the glimmers?
C: firbolg kin who pray to the sky. they look at the glimmers and see death itself, so they crusade against the whole kingdom | sky worshipping firbolg kin. to them the glimmers are death given light, and they want every last one put out
B: they are firbolg blood. kin over strangers, every time | the azure are ours by blood. the glimmers are not ours by anything
C: the glimmers were in sheeroe before the pass came out of the mist. we walked into their kingdom, not the other way round | sheeroe was the glimmers' home before any of us found the road up. we are the guests there
B: guests do not get breathed on by the host. their king is eighty levels of no welcome | their king breathes on everyone who visits. that is not much of a host
C: so would you, if a crusade came over your hill with spears | anyone breathes on a crusade. ask the albs at our keeps
D: think of it from the azure side. the firbolg lost mag tuired, in the old tale, to a people who came out of the mist | from the firbolg side it makes sense. their kin lost this land once to a people who came in a mist with magic
D: now a kingdom of strange lights walks out of the bog mist again. of course the azure grabbed their spears first | and one day a glowing kingdom steps out of the cullen mist. if i were firbolg i would reach for a spear too
E: wait, the tuatha came out of the mist too? | so the firbolg have seen this before?
C: so the old tale says. mist and dark cloud, then magic, then the firbolg lost the land and kept only connacht | in the old songs, yes. the tuatha came in mist and beat the firbolg at the first mag tuired
B: then the azure are right. once burned, twice careful | so the azure learned their lesson and we should listen
~ 15
C: or they are fighting the last war again, against folk who were never part of it. the glimmers did not come with the tuatha | or they are refighting a battle from before the realms against people who never did them harm
D: they even took the sky for their names. azure dsaldor, azure coseal, azure thror, all wearing the same blue word | the azure name themselves for the sky they pray to. dsaldor, coseal, thror, every one of them azure first
E: so which side gives better xp? | which side do i kill, then?
D: both. the azure camps never run dry and the glimmers pay about the same per pull. my sword is neutral | both, honestly. i camp the azure until they bore me and then the glimmers. xp has no politics
B: then pick the side that does not glow in the dark. at least the azure let you sleep | the azure at least do not keep you up all night glowing
C: i stay out of the crusade. the otherworld has a long memory, and it remembers who put its lights out | leave me out of it. the folk under the hills keep grudges for a thousand years, the old tales are full of it
A: so the realm is split, as usual | as usual, three opinions and no answer
?B: four, if you count maldahar the glimmer prince. he has strong views on the subject | ask maldahar the glimmer prince. i bet he has an opinion

# ---- Do we trust the Sylvan ---------------------------------------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_sylvan
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_trust_sylvan
@role B type=NoLifer levels=30-50
@role C type=Explorer
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Crafter
A: {q} honest question: does hibernia trust the sylvan, or are we only glad of the help? | {q} do we really trust the sylvan, or do we just need them?
?D: the sylvan, the tree people? i saw one in tir na nog and thought it was decoration | the sylvan like the race? i nearly hit one thinking it was a tree mob
B: they came out of hy brasil asking for swords against the fomorians. anyone who fights balor's lot is my friend | they asked us for help against the fomor and tur suil. an enemy of balor is a friend of mine
C: they let us onto hy brasil, an island every fisherman called a story. without them it would still be a tale in a bard's mouth | thanks to them we walk on hy brasil. we called that island a myth for as long as anyone can remember
E: trees with long memories, though. i cut bow staves from saplings every day. some of those saplings had cousins | i am a fletcher. i have cut more young trees than i can count. i do not want a sylvan doing that sum
B: we have always been friends of trees. our oldest letters, the ogham, are tree names: beith the birch, luis the rowan, fearn the alder | ogham, our oldest writing, has a tree for every sign. birch, rowan, alder, willow. we wrote in trees long before they walked
C: and look at the ones that went wrong. corrupt sylvan on the isle, sylvanshades in galladoria, hurionthex wearing any face it likes | but some of them turned. there are corrupt sylvan in hy brasil and sylvanshades in galladoria. that was their own kin once
D: what turned them? | why did some of them go bad?
B: olcasgean's roots. the titan banished to world's end pushes his roots under the isle and the rot climbs into anything that grows | the roots of olcasgean. he was banished to world's end, and his roots taint the whole isle. whatever grows there drinks it
C: and what is a sylvan but a thing that grows there? if the rot takes some, it can take the ones who talk to us too | which is the worry. the rot gets into growing things, and every sylvan who shook our hand grows there too
B: so we cut the root at galladoria and the problem goes with it | then the answer is galladoria. kill the root, keep the friends
E: the hard part is the kill olcasgean bit. he is eighty seven | olcasgean is level eighty seven. easy to say kill the root from a tavern
~ 15
C: there is an older tale too. the bards say five great trees of this land fell in one age, and nobody ever said who cut them | our bards sing of five great trees that all fell in the same age. the songs never say who swung the axe
B: and you think the sylvan know? | you think the sylvan remember who did it?
C: i think they remember everything. a people made of wood would not forget a thing like that | a tree that lived through that does not forget who held the axe
E: then i will buy my staves from the merchant for a while | i might buy my bow staves from a vendor for a bit
B: trust is a strong word. i trust them for as long as balor is the bigger problem | i trust them as long as tur suil stands. after that we will see
?D: that is how my guild treats me too | sounds like most alliances i have heard of
A: friends for now, with an axe kept under the bed | friends for now. just do not cut any saplings near domnann

# ---- The hydra under Fomor ----------------------------------------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_hydra
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_hydra_heads
@role B type=Crafter
@role C type=NoLifer levels=40-50
@role D type=Daredevil,Explorer levels=30-50
@role E type=* levels=5-25
A: {q} is it true the heads on myrddraxis heal each other? | {q} heard the hydra under fomor heals its own heads. real, or raid talk?
?E: myrddraxis? | what is a myrddraxis?
C: a hydra at the bottom of fomor, the fomorian city on hy brasil. level eighty, and every raid story from there says the heads mend each other | the hydra in the deepest part of fomor. eighty levels and many heads, and every story i have heard says the heads heal each other
B: forget the heads. what i care about is the scale. supposedly it never needs repair | the heads are someone else's problem. i want to know if the scale that never breaks is real
C: i have heard of it a dozen times and never once seen one worn | heard about it plenty. never seen it worn, never seen it sold
B: then someone is hoarding them. the best drops always sit in somebody else's bank | so someone has one and keeps quiet. that is how the good drops stay rare
D: the story i heard: a raid of fifty fought it for an hour and came out with nothing but a bag of nuts | a friend of a friend was on a raid of fifty that fought it for an hour. they left with nuts. actual nuts
B: nuts is a drop. and the loot on myrddraxis was changed in the autumn patch, so the old stories may be out of date | everyone laughs about the nuts, but the myrddraxis loot was redone last autumn. the scale may be new
E: why would a hydra live at the bottom of a fomorian city anyway? | what is a hydra doing in fomor?
C: in the old tales the fomorians were sea raiders, half monster themselves. a many headed thing under their city suits them | the fomor of the old tales came from the sea and looked like nightmares. a hydra in their cellar is about right
D: in the far southern tale you sear each neck with fire as the head comes off, or two grow back. maybe nobody here has tried | the southern story says burn each neck as you cut it. maybe the heads do not heal, they just grow back
C: we are not a realm of fire mages. our casters throw light and void. good luck searing a neck with that | hibernia has no fire school worth the name. sear a neck with a sun spell and see what happens
D: then all the heads at once, the way the raid leaders say. take them down together or not at all | so all heads at the same time. that is the advice i keep hearing
~ 15
B: and the other big named down there? anurigunda, nogoribando. any word on what they carry? | there are other named in fomor too, anurigunda and nogoribando. any good drop stories about them?
C: the only one i know is krackenschtein, and only because of the name | krackenschtein. somebody down there has a sense of humour, or a very strange mother
E: krackenschtein is not a real name, you made that up | that cannot be a real monster
C: real as rain, level seventy, and he does not laugh at his name either | he is real, level seventy, and very tired of jokes about it
B: every famous drop was a rumour first. when i get the scale, it goes on my vendor at a price that makes you cry | rumours are how every good drop starts. the scale is out there and one day it sits on my vendor
A: i will believe it when one is for sale | sell me one and i will believe you
D: if you ever get one, show it before you sell it. i want proof | put it on display in your house first. i want to see it with my own eyes

# ---- Are bards overpowered (and were they always) -----------------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_bards
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_bards_overpowered
@role B class=Bard
@role C type=NoLifer levels=35-50
@role D class=Eldritch
@role E type=* levels=5-25
A: {q} honest question, are bards overpowered or just loved? | {q} the other realms all say our bards are overpowered. are they right?
C: speed, mez, heals and a harp. yes. every alb in emain would say so with his last breath | speed, mez and heals in one kilt. that is three classes, and every enemy in emain macha knows it
B: and we die first in every fight because of it. first name on every target list in the frontier | so we die first. ask anyone who plays a bard in a keep fight: the albs go for us before the casters
C: you get rezzed first too, because nobody can run home without your speed | and you get the first rez, because the group cannot leave without your speed song
?E: why does a bard get all that and a ranger does not? | how come bards get so much?
B: because bards were always more than singers here. in the old days a poet sat near the king, and a king feared a poet's satire | in the old days a poet ranked near the king. a good satire could ruin a king, and everyone knew it
B: when bres the half-fomorian king starved his guests, the poet cairbre sang a satire and red blotches rose on his face. bres lost the throne | the first satire in this land, the old tale says: cairbre sang against stingy king bres and his face broke out in blotches. bres fell
D: so this realm has been run by people with harps since before the realms. explains a lot about tir na nog | so the realm has always been ruled by musicians. explains the city politics
B: and mez is nothing new. the dagda's harp played three strains: the crying strain, the laughing strain, the sleeping strain. we kept one | the dagda's own harp had three strains, sorrow, laughter and sleep. a bard's mez is only the sleep strain, handed down
C: the dagda used the sleep strain to walk out of a fomorian hall. bards use it to walk out of a fight they started | the dagda put a hall of fomorians to sleep and walked out with his harp. you mez a group and leave me holding the pull
D: as an eldritch i would trade my nukes for that speed song tomorrow | i would give up my best nuke for that speed song, and i like my nuke
B: then reroll. the kilt is waiting, and so is the drum | then roll one. the kilt is on the rack in tir na nog
D: no thanks, i like standing at the back where the albs cannot see me | i will keep my robe. bards are the first thing the enemy looks for
~ 15
E: do the albs and the norse not have bards? | is it true the other realms have bards too?
C: they have minstrels and skalds. a skald sings about killing you, a minstrel sings about himself. ours sing you to sleep and then heal you | albion has minstrels and midgard has skalds. good fighters, both. neither one heals you after
B: and when the milesians first came ashore the tuatha raised a druid wind against the ships. their poet amergin sang, and the wind lay down | the old tale says the first poet of the milesians calmed a druid wind with one song. bards have been overpowered since the first ship
C: you hear that? even the legends say overpowered | there it is, overpowered since the old tales
A: i will take that as a yes | overpowered it is then, but beloved
?B: overpowered is the word people use for a class they cannot catch | we are not overpowered. we are just fast enough that you never see the rest

# ---- Is Muire Tomb haunted ----------------------------------------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_muire
@levels 10-40
@scene lore_hib_muire_haunted
@role B type=Explorer
@role C type=Leveler
@role D type=* levels=10-20
@role E type=Socialite
A: {q} is muire tomb actually haunted, or is it just dark and full of undead? | {q} anyone else think muire tomb is haunted for real?
C: it is a tomb full of undead. haunted is the whole point, that is why we go there for xp | every tomb is haunted, that is what makes it a tomb. good xp in the twenties though
B: it is more than that. the old lord of the muire house went mad and killed his own household. that is why they are all down there | the realm's own story: the lord of the muire house went mad and murdered his whole family. they did not choose that tomb
?D: wait, the muires are a family? i thought it was just a name on the door | the mobs are a family? i thought they just shared a name
B: look at the names. quillan muire, beare muire, kacey muire, alsandair muire, even the muire herbalist. a whole house, laid down together | read the cons. quillan, beare, kacey, alsandair, all muires. there is even the family herbalist down there with them
C: and the muire hero still guards the rest. at level twenty one he guards them very poorly | the muire hero guards them still. i have killed him so many times i feel bad about it
E: you do realise that makes you the haunting, right? | so you are the thing that haunts them. congratulations
B: what i want to know is why the lord went mad. lords do not just wake up and kill their families | nobody says why. a lord does not murder his house over breakfast. something did that to him
C: some lords do. read the old tales, half the kings in them end at a feast with knives | have you read the old songs? half the kings in them end their own families
B: my guess is a geis. the old heroes carried bans they could not break. break your geis and your luck turns on you, and your mind | a geis, i think. the old tales are full of them, a sacred ban. break it and everything you love turns on you
D: what is a geis? | a geis?
B: a ban laid on you for life. cuchulainn had a geis never to eat dog and one never to refuse a meal on the road. that is what killed him | something you must never do. cuchulainn could not eat dog and could not refuse hospitality. his enemies offered him dog meat
C: and you think the lord ate the wrong supper? there is a geas bound protector out in the bog of cullen, go and ask him | lovely story. there is a geas bound protector in the bog of cullen if you want to ask one in person
~ 15
E: the one rule i know is never whistle in there. the family wakes up | rule one of muire tomb: no whistling
B: the old folk say the banshee keens only for the old families. somebody keened for the muires once, and i think she never stopped | the banshee wails only for the old houses, the old folk say. i am sure i heard her down in the lower halls
C: that was a groupmate's pet. i have done that place a hundred times and heard nothing but rats and my healer | that was the wind in the halls, or a pet. i have levelled there more than anyone and it is only noise
B: then why do you only go in with a full group? | then why do you never solo it?
C: because a group kills faster, not because of your banshee | because the xp is better in a group. nothing to do with ghosts
?D: i soloed the top hall once and something followed me out. i did not turn round | i heard footsteps behind me in there, with nobody behind me
A: noted. group up, no whistling, and say something nice to the muire hero | ok. full group, no whistling, and be polite to the hero
E: and leave the deepest room for last. i want to see who flinches | i will hum the whole way down. somebody has to test it

# ---- Hy Brasil from the Cliffs of Moher ---------------------------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_moher
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_seen_from_moher
@role B type=NoLifer levels=30-50
@role C type=Explorer
@role D type=* levels=5-25
@role E type=Socialite,Daredevil
A: {q} could you really see hy brasil from the cliffs of moher, before the sylvan showed us the way? | {q} my granda swore hy brasil showed off moher every seven years. old fisherman talk, or not?
B: the old tale says it shows once every seven years off the west coast, and then the mist takes it back | that is the old story: every seventh year the mist lifts off the western sea and the island is there
C: plenty of fishing folk in connla and tir urphost swore they saw it. and then it turned out to be real | the fishermen in connla swore by it for years. and then the sylvan came and the island was real
B: or you can see fog from moher, and fog looks like whatever you want it to | fog off those cliffs looks like an island if you have been drinking long enough
D: wait, hy brasil is the place with the tree people, right? | hy brasil is the island where the sylvan live? that is what they meant?
C: the very same. long thought to be a myth, and now people bind there. from a story for children to a bind point in one season | same isle. it was a children's tale one year and the next we had a ship and a bind stone there
B: lucky guess by a lot of thirsty fishermen. say an island is out west long enough and one day something turns up | say there is an island west of you every evening for a hundred years and one day you will be right
C: the old sea charts drew it too, a round island cut through the middle by a channel. a lucky guess with a shape? | the oldest charts drew hy brasil round, with a river through the middle. that is not a guess, that is a description
B: the same charts drew sea serpents and men with dogs' heads. a mapmaker draws what sells | those charts had serpents in every corner. drawn to sell, not to sail
E: honestly the serpents might be real too. ever swum off vigilant rock? | i would not bet against the serpents. something big lives under that sea
C: and look at the names on our own cliffs. aoife walks moher, and in the old tale aoife turned the children of lir into swans | the named on moher have old tale names. aoife is there, and in the old song aoife turned lir's children into swans
C: there is even a bran there, bran the giant. in the old voyage bran sailed west to the isle of women and could not come home | and bran the giant stands on those cliffs too. bran is the man in the old tale who sailed west to the otherworld isles
B: and the grey man, who is just fog with a name. the old folk call him the fog that walks, right where your granda was looking | and the grey man, who is the fog itself if you ask a sailor. maybe what your granda saw was him
~ 15
D: if you could see it every seven years, why did nobody just sail there? | so why did we need the sylvan, if fishermen saw it?
B: the old tale says you could never reach it unless you fixed it with fire. put a burning arrow into it and it could not sink away again | the trick in the old story: throw fire onto the island before the mist closes, and it can never vanish again
C: and connla on the estuary carries the name of a prince in the old tale who stepped into a boat of glass with an otherworld woman | connla the fair, in the tale, was called away over the western sea in a crystal boat. we named a fishing town after him
E: so the fishermen of connla have been staring west for a reason | no wonder the connla folk look out to sea all day
A: so what do they see out there now that hy brasil is found? | and what do the fishermen see from moher now?
C: something still. allta fearann turned up not long ago, one more place nobody knew. i think the sea has another left | they still see shapes. allta fearann was found after hy brasil. i would bet there is one more out there
B: when your next island turns up, i will buy the boat | i will believe in the next island when it has a bind stone
E: dare stands: i stand on moher with a fire arrow every evening until something holds still | i will keep a fire arrow ready on moher. if anything shows up, it stays

# ---- Juggernaut and the con colours -------------------------------------------------------------
@topic lore_hib_juggernaut
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_juggernaut_con
@role B type=Daredevil
@role C type=NoLifer levels=40-50
@role D type=Explorer
@role E type=* levels=5-25
A: {q} is juggernaut really as strong as people say, or is it all fear? | {q} heard juggernaut hits way harder than his con says
?E: juggernaut? | who is juggernaut?
C: a named in the cursed forest. listed at seventy five, and i never met anyone who believed the listing | he walks the cursed forest. seventy five on paper. nobody who has met him believes the paper
B: i say he is soft. a big brute with legs. someone just has to commit | he is a big lump with legs. we could take him with a real group
C: i watched three full groups try him and i watched three full groups run home | i have seen what he does to full groups. i was one of the corpses
B: those groups had no bard. no mez, no speed, no chance | those groups pulled badly, that is all
D: the stranger part is where he lives. the zone story says the faerie court is rumoured to walk the cursed forest | what bothers me is the place. the cursed forest is where the faerie court is said to wander
E: the faerie court? like fairies? i only know the werewolves | fairies in the cursed forest? i only go there for the werewolf camps
D: the old tales call it the fairy host, riding the wind. when dust whirls on a still road, the old folk say the host is passing | the slua si, the fairy host. a dust whirl on a calm day is the host riding by, the old folk say, and you bow to it
D: so why would the court share a forest with juggernaut? unless he is theirs, a warden on some door | so what is the court doing in a wood with juggernaut in it? maybe he guards something of theirs
C: or the court keeps out of his way like everyone else. i have seen the faerie queen in there and she keeps her distance | i have seen the faerie queen in the cursed forest. even she gives him a wide berth
B: the names in that forest are something. uafas capall, the horror horse. skeaghshee, the fairy thorn. luriquay. and then him | look at the named around him. uafas capall means horror horse in the old tongue. skeaghshee is the fairy thorn. and then juggernaut
D: everything in that wood has an old tale name except juggernaut. he does not belong. something brought him there | every named there wears an old name except him. that tells me he came from somewhere else
~ 15
C: i think the same. maybe the con lies about him because he is not from here. the con is made for our own monsters | the con colour is wrong on him. maybe because cons are made for our monsters and he is a stranger
E: can con colours lie? i trust them for everything | wait, cons can be wrong? that is how i pick fights
C: for a few named, yes. there is one in that same forest with two different levels listed, and both feel wrong | for him and a few others in that forest. one named there has two levels listed and neither is right
B: fine. i still say he goes down one day. get him to half and i buy the round | dare stands. a full group, a bard, and he goes down
D: if he ever does, look at what he carries. a stranger carries strange things | i want to see what he drops. if it is from the old tales i will eat my map
A: i will watch from the werewolf camp and cheer | i will be at the werewolf camp, as far away as i can be
?C: bring the rez. bring two | i will keep your bind spot warm

# =========================================================================================
# Tale scenes: one bot tells a legend, the others interrupt
# =========================================================================================

@topic lore_tale_sheeroe
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_tale_sheeroe_pass
@role A type=NoLifer levels=35-50
@role B type=* levels=5-25
@role C type=Explorer
@role D type=Socialite,Leveler
A: {q} sit down, newer folk. here is how the sheeroe pass came out of the mist | {q} the tale of the sheeroe pass, for anyone who never heard it. it is a good one
?B: please. i only know sheeroe as the glowing place nobody lets me near | go on, all i know is the glow
A: the rangers knew the bog of cullen like their own hands. every pool, every hummock, every crow that badb sends out to look | in those days the rangers had walked every foot of cullen. they knew every pool and every one of badb's birds
A: then one grey morning the mist came up off the bog thicker than they had ever seen it, and it stayed | then came a morning when the mist rose and would not lift. the bog went white, and nobody went in
C: same as the tuatha in the old tale. they came in a mist that hid the sun for three days | that is how the tuatha came, in the oldest story. a mist that hid the sun three days
A: when it cleared, a pass ran up into the hills where there had been none. old stones, worn smooth, as if it had always waited | when it lifted, there was a pass in the hills no ranger had ever walked. stones worn smooth, like it had always been there
D: or the rangers had a bad week and missed a hill. it happens to me in silvermine | or they never looked properly. i lose whole roads in silvermine
A: the first rangers up saw lights drifting on the slopes, little glowing things that watched them, and then came at them | the first ones up saw lights on the ridge. they called them glimmers, and the glimmers did not like visitors
B: so that is where the glimmers came from | the glimmers! i have seen them from the road
A: an elven sage told the realm what it was: a kingdom of the otherworld, ruled by a glimmer king named cuuldurach | the sages told the rangers the truth. it was a kingdom of the otherworld, and its king was cuuldurach
C: and the azure found it just as fast. firbolg kin, sky worshippers, sure the glimmers were death itself, and they declared a crusade | then came the azure, firbolg kin who pray to the sky, sworn to burn out every glimmer they could find
D: so the moral is, if a new road appears, there is a dragon at the end of it | lesson learned. a new road means a new dragon
A: the moral is the mist gives back what it hid. it did it once in cullen. i keep watching the bog for the second time | the moral is the mist opens when it likes. it opened once in cullen, and i keep an eye on it in case it opens again
C: you and me both. i ride past the cullen fold every week and wait | i check the bog of cullen every time i ride by

@topic lore_tale_muire
@levels 10-50
@scene lore_hib_tale_muire_madness
@role A type=NoLifer,Explorer levels=25-50
@role B type=* levels=10-20
@role C type=Leveler
@role D type=Socialite,Daredevil
A: {q} anyone want the tale of the muire house? the way my first guild told it, anyway | {q} here is how my old guild told the story of muire tomb. take it as a story
?B: yes please. i camp there and nobody tells me anything | go on, i am in there most nights
A: the muires were an old house with good land and a lord people liked. a family herbalist, a hero sworn to them, children in the hall | they were an old family, the muires, with a lord, a hero sworn to the house, a herbalist and a hall full of kin
A: the story says the lord rode out hunting one winter and came back quiet. he would not eat, and he would not sit by the fire | then one winter the lord came home from the hills changed. he would not eat at the table or look anyone in the eye
C: that is the part nobody agrees on. one version says he found something in the hills, another says he lost something | in the version i heard he met a woman on the road. every version has something in the hills
A: some say he broke a geis, some say a woman of the sidhe touched him. my guild said the amadan dubh brushed his sleeve | my old guild swore it was the dark fool, the amadan dubh. one touch and a man is never right again
D: lovely. so a fairy ruined him and his family paid. typical | so the fool touched the lord and the whole house paid for it. classic
A: one night he barred the hall and killed them all. the herbalist, the children, quillan, beare, every muire under the roof | and one night he barred the doors and murdered every one of them. quillan, beare, kacey, alsandair, the herbalist, all
B: and the muire hero? | where was the hero when it happened?
A: out on the road. he came home to a silent hall and swore he would guard them for ever. he is still at it | too late, the story says. he found them dead, swore to guard them, and he guards them still
C: and every group in the realm kills him for xp. that part makes me feel a bit bad | and we kill him for xp every night. he never gets a rest
A: and the lord? nobody knows. my old guild swore he is down at the very bottom of the tomb, still awake | what became of the lord, nobody says. some say the bog took him, my guild said he is at the bottom of the tomb
D: so that is why you do not whistle in there. he is listening | that explains the no whistling rule. he hears it

@topic lore_tale_balor
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_tale_balor_eye
@role A class=Bard,Druid
@role B type=* levels=5-25
@role C type=NoLifer levels=35-50
@role D type=Daredevil,Socialite
A: {q} want the old tale of balor's eye? worth knowing before anyone goes near tur suil | {q} story time. the tale of balor's eye, the way the bards sing it in tir na nog
?B: yes, everyone says do not look him in the eye and nobody says why | go on. i keep hearing about his eye
A: balor was king of the fomorians. as a boy he spied on his father's druids brewing a spell, and the fumes went into one eye | balor of the fomor was a boy when he watched druids at their cauldron. the fumes got into one eye, and it went bad for ever
A: after that whatever the eye looked on died. in battle four men raised the lid with a polished handle, and armies fell | from then on the eye killed whatever it saw. it took four men with a handle to lift the lid, and then whole hosts died
D: so the fomorian army marches behind a man they can never let turn round | must have been a nervous job, standing behind him
A: a druid foretold that his own grandson would kill him. so he shut his only daughter away where no man could ever reach her | then a druid told him his grandson would be his death, so he locked his daughter eithne away on an island
A: a man reached her anyway, and she bore a son. that son was lugh, and he was raised far away among the tuatha | someone reached her all the same, and her son was lugh, raised far from balor among the tuatha
B: lugh, like lug's spear? our relic? | lug like the spear in cruachan?
C: the same. in the tale no battle went against the one who held lugh's spear. ours sits in cruachan and the albs keep trying to borrow it | the same name. in the old tale that spear could not lose a battle. ours rests in cruachan and the norse would love to take it
A: at the second mag tuired the lid came up, and lugh put a sling stone through the eye so hard it went out the back of balor's head | at the second battle of mag tuired, when the lid rose, lugh slung a stone that drove the eye out through the back of balor's skull
A: and the eye, still open, fell looking at the fomorian ranks behind him. his own gaze killed his own army | the eye landed facing the fomorians, and its gaze killed his own host. that is how the fomor lost the war
C: fine story, except our balor sits in tur suil right now at level eighty one, eye and all | lovely, except there is a balor alive in tur suil at eighty one and nobody has put a stone through anything
C: the sylvan say his eye opened once on hy brasil and a whole valley burned. that is why the vale of balor looks the way it does | the sylvan tell that his eye opened over the isle once and scorched a whole valley. that is how the vale got his name
A: so if you go to tur suil, never meet his eye, and remember who his grandson was | so: never meet his eye, aim for the lid, and remember who his grandson was

@topic lore_tale_oisin
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_tale_oisin_return
@role A type=Socialite,NoLifer
@role B type=* levels=5-25
@role C type=Leveler
@role D type=Explorer
A: {q} since people keep mentioning the land of youth, here is the tale of oisin, the way my gran told it | {q} the story of oisin and niamh, for anyone who never heard it. the short version
A: oisin was fionn's son, a poet and a fighter of the fianna. one day a woman rode out of the west on a white horse, niamh of the golden hair | oisin, son of fionn, was hunting with the fianna when niamh of the golden hair rode to them out of the sea mist on a white horse
B: who was she? | wait, who is niamh?
A: a king's daughter from tir na nog, the land of youth. she had come for oisin alone, and he got up behind her and they rode over the waves | a princess of the land of youth. she asked oisin to come with her, and he climbed up behind her and they rode out across the sea
C: just like that? no goodbye to fionn? | left his father and his band for a girl on a horse. bold
A: in tir na nog nobody aged and nobody hungered, and three years went by like a summer. then he grew homesick for the fianna | three years he lived there, feasting, never a day older. then he longed to see fionn and the fianna again
A: niamh lent him the white horse with one warning: whatever you do, never set foot on the ground | she let him ride home on the white horse, with one warning: never let your foot touch the soil of the mortal world
D: here it comes | you know where this is going
A: home was strange. the fianna were long gone, their fort was grass, and men knew fionn only from songs. three hundred years had passed | he found the fianna dead and their halls fallen, and small men who knew fionn only from the bards. three hundred years had gone by
A: he saw men struggling with a stone and leaned from the saddle to help. the girth broke, he fell, and as he touched the ground he aged | some men could not shift a boulder, so he leaned down to lift it. the girth snapped, he hit the ground, and he was old in a breath
B: that is horrible. and the horse? | oh no. what happened to the horse?
A: the white horse turned and ran back into the sea, and oisin never saw niamh or the land of youth again | the horse galloped back into the waves without him, and that was the end of tir na nog for oisin
C: which is why i always check my girth before a long ride | lesson: check the saddle before you visit the otherworld
D: and why i think the door under the hill is real. you do not give a warning that exact about a place that does not exist | i always took it as a warning. you only warn people about doors that exist

@topic lore_tale_olcasgean
@levels 1-50
@scene lore_hib_tale_olcasgean_banished
@role A type=Explorer,NoLifer levels=30-50
@role B type=* levels=5-25
@role C type=Leveler
@role D type=Crafter
A: {q} the sylvan in domnann have a tale about why olcasgean ended up at world's end. want it? | {q} heard a sylvan tell why olcasgean was banished. it is their tale, not mine, but it is a good one
B: who is olcasgean? | olcasgean? never heard of that one
A: a titan, a child of gaea, old as the first hills. he waits at the end of galladoria, and his roots run under all of hy brasil | the titan at the bottom of galladoria. a child of gaea, older than tuatha and fomor, with roots under the whole isle
A: the way the sylvan tell it, he wanted the whole isle for himself. every tree to lean toward him, every root to drink from his | the sylvan say he was not content to be one old power among many. he wanted every root on the isle to answer to him
C: sounds like a guild leader i had once | so a titan who could not share. i have grouped with that
A: the other old powers would not have it. they could not kill a child of gaea, so they drove him to the farthest edge, where the land runs out | the others could not destroy him, so they banished him to the very edge of the isle, world's end, as far as the land goes
D: how do you banish a thing with roots? he just grows back | banish a tree? he would put down roots wherever they left him
A: that was the trouble. he did put down roots, and they crept back under the isle toward everything he lost. that is the rot out there | exactly what went wrong. his roots crept back under hy brasil, and wherever they reach, the sylvan sicken and turn
B: is that what the corrupt sylvan are? | so the corrupt sylvan are his doing?
A: so the sylvan believe. galladoria is where the roots lead, and hurionthex down there wears any shape it likes, so trust nothing that smiles | the sylvan think so. galladoria is full of things grown from him, like hurionthex, who wears whatever face will fool you
C: and this is why i level in the old zones | i will stick to the cursed forest werewolves, thank you
D: my old crafting teacher would never buy wood cut near world's end. said it warps in the bag, and i think i know why now | an old crafter belief: wood from world's end warps toward the sea. i always thought it was a joke until now
A: elro the ancient waits out at world's end too. i like to think he is one of the jailers they left behind, still on watch | and elro the ancient is out at world's end. i always wondered if he was the jailer, still keeping watch

